      <h3>Cost Considerations</h3>
      <p>We quote both plants per build rather than publish a price, so ask for the two side by side. Either way the decision should not rest on purchase price alone. Consider these factors:</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cost per cubic yard:</strong> M300 has lower operating cost per cy processed</li>
        <li><strong>Infrastructure costs:</strong> M300 requires more substantial power and water systems</li>
        <li><strong>Shipping:</strong> the M200 ships in a single 40' container. The M300 does not containerise and needs specialised transport, which is the bigger logistical difference between them.</li>
        <li><strong>Site preparation:</strong> M300 needs larger, more level site area</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Site Requirements: A Critical Factor</h3>
      <p>Our DRC client initially wanted the M300 for maximum throughput, but site constraints made the M200 the better choice:</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Access roads:</strong> Can they handle the heavier M300 components?</li>
        <li><strong>Site size:</strong> M300 needs 150' x 80' vs M200's 120' x 60'</li>
        <li><strong>Power:</strong> can you run diesel on site, or do you have grid supply?</li>
        <li><strong>Water source:</strong> Can you supply 1,500 GPM consistently?</li>
        <li><strong>Soil conditions:</strong> Rocky ground may limit actual throughput regardless of plant size</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Which Should You Choose?</h3>
      <p><strong>Choose the M200 if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>You're processing 150-250 tons per hour of material</li>
        <li>Your site has moderate power/water infrastructure</li>
        <li>You want proven reliability with lower initial investment</li>
        <li>Shipping costs are a significant factor</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Choose the M300 if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>You need to process 250+ cy/hr consistently</li>
        <li>You have robust power and water infrastructure</li>
        <li>Your operation runs 24/7 and needs maximum capacity</li>
        <li>Site preparation and shipping costs aren't limiting factors</li>
      </ul>
      
